videos.script:
Picture two phones side by side. Their screens are the same size, but one has three times as many pixels across. {{phones}}

A button drawn forty-eight pixels wide is a comfortable target on the first, and a speck on the second. So nobody on a phone measures in pixels. {{speck}} Android measures in density-independent pixels, dp. iOS measures in points. React Native uses its own units, which are the platform's. {{units}} Each one is roughly the same physical size on every screen, and the system turns it into real pixels.

In this lesson you learn the sum behind that, and the five density buckets Android sorts its screens into. {{buckets}} A short program works out what forty-eight dp becomes on each of them, and on an iPhone at two and three times. {{program}} Then the price of an image: drawn once as a vector, or saved again in every size a screen might ask for. {{assets}}
